WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.962
NULL values require special attention in aggregation.

00:00:03.962 --> 00:00:05.792
In the admissions table, an ongoing admission has no discharge time yet.

00:00:05.792 --> 00:00:11.197
That means we cannot calculate a completed length of stay for that row.

00:00:11.197 --> 00:00:14.047
AVG ignores NULL expressions rather than treating them as zero.

00:00:14.047 --> 00:00:20.077
That behavior is useful here because an unfinished stay is not a zero-day stay.

00:00:20.077 --> 00:00:23.767
Run the example and look at the completed length-of-stay calculation.

00:00:23.767 --> 00:00:26.732
Think about which admissions actually contribute to the average.

00:00:26.732 --> 00:00:30.277
Whenever you report an average, also think about the sample size.

00:00:30.277 --> 00:00:35.527
An average based on eight completed admissions means something different from an average based on all ten

00:00:35.527 --> 00:00:40.007
admission rows. Missing values can change which records are included in the measure.

00:00:40.007 --> 00:00:43.632
Always name the thing being counted or averaged.

00:00:43.632 --> 00:00:47.267
COUNT star counts rows, COUNT of a column ignores NULL in that column,

00:00:47.267 --> 00:00:49.982
and COUNT DISTINCT answers a different question again.

00:00:49.982 --> 00:00:57.277
In dashboards, a vague label such as Count can hide major analytical errors; use labels such as admissions,

00:00:57.277 --> 00:00:57.920
patients, or distinct orders.
